  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of rain through tonight with a coastal\nlow before drier weather returns early next week.\n\nRegional radar imagery across the Mid-Atlantic shows light\nreturns lifting northward across the area. However, much of this\nis likely struggling to reach the ground given substantial low-\nlevel dry air. Local aircraft soundings show a pocket of dry air\nsitting between 850-650 mb. Some moistening is now underway well\nsouth of I-66 as skies have become overcast in the 2,200 to\n3,000 foot level. Looking down toward the Carolinas, this is\nwhere the more impressive rainfall signature is, particularly\ntoward the North Carolina-South Carolina coastal border. Models\ngenerally agree on light activity continuing to lift northward\nthroughout the morning. It remains to be seen if any of this\nmakes it to the surface though.\n\nOvercast skies will be prevalent today while winds gradually\nturn more north to northeasterly. This is in response to the\napproach of a wavy frontal system which straddles the Carolina\nto Delmarva coast. Despite the rain chances today, forecast\nhighs should reach the low/mid 60s, with 50s over mountain\nlocations. In terms of the expected rainfall, there has\ncertainly been a downward trend in forecast amounts. At this\npoint, areas east of the Blue Ridge can expect 0.10 to 0.25\ninches, with even lighter amounts across the Shenandoah Valley.\nThe 00Z REFS ensembles show the most concentrated activity \ncrossing the I-95 metro areas between around 6-10 PM this \nevening before shifting toward the Eastern Shore after midnight.\n\nAs the wavy coastal system pulls out to sea, a cold front\npressing across the Mississippi Valley will reach the area on\nMonday morning. While most stay dry, some northwesterly aided\nupslope rain showers are possible along/west of the Allegheny\nFront. Further to the east, the resultant downsloping flow will\nmake for one more mild day as highs push into the mid 60s to low\n70s. The cool spots will be across the mountains with Monday's\nhighs in the upper 40s to 50s. Model guidance shows a more\npronounced drop in theta-e values which comes with falling\ntemperatures and dew points. Additionally, this comes with a\nsurge of northwesterly winds with evening gusts up to 20 to 25\nmph. A seasonably chilly night lies ahead with lows in the\nmid/upper 20s west of the Blue Ridge, to widespread 30s off to\nthe east.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cooler conditions are expected next week, with a\ncoastal low forecast to track up the East Coast on Thanksgiving\nDay.\n\nAfter a chilly couple of days Tue-Wed under strong Canadian high \npressure, the next low pressure area to impact the area on \nThanksgiving Day has trended slower, closer to the coast, \nwetter, and windier according to the latest set of ensemble \nguidance. \n\nThe parent 500 mb trough has trended deeper with a closed 500 mb low \nforecast to develop over the Southeast U.S. coast Thanksgiving Day, \nlifting to the VA Tidewater region by Thu evening. This results in \nthe associated low pressure center to lift north significantly \nslower than in previous runs resulting in stronger winds along the \ncoast and heavier precip with less cold air available in the \nmountains for accumulating snow. The system looks to lift northeast \nof the area by late Thu night or early Fri with precip ending. A \nbrief period of upslope flow and cold temperatures may support light \nsnow showers across the mountains Fri, but favorable cyclonic flow \nlooks to last too short in duration for any appreciable snow \namounts. Weak high pressure builds over the area for next weekend \nwith seasonable temperatures for late November.",
